## The pipeline (this context's slice)

```yaml
engineering_pipeline:
  0_sync:      design-system-engineer regenerates coded tokens from the published Figma DS; diff
  1_components: build/extend {{COMPONENT_LIB}} components + Storybook stories; token-bound (design-system-engineer)
  1b_parity:   PARITY GATE — Storybook screenshot vs the Figma source; zero unbound, zero drift
  2_publish:   version + publish {{COMPONENT_LIB}} so prototype AND app consume the same version
  3_app:       compose the shared library into the validated screens; wire nav/state/platform (developer)
  4_verify:    {{VERIFY_GATE}} gate — {{VERIFY_GATE_DESC}}
  5_build:     build the {{DEPLOY_UNIT}}; verify by artifact (release-engineer)
  6_ship:      deploy to {{RELEASE_TARGET}}; CONFIRM by re-reading {{RELEASE_CONFIRM}} (release-engineer)
  7_record:    record version + commit + where it landed + what shipped
  feedback:
    ds_gap:      "{{COMPONENT_LIB}} needs a token/component the Figma DS lacks" → {{DS_REPO}}, before coding
    lib_gap:     "a screen needs a component the library lacks" → design-system-engineer, before app build
    flow_back:   "the flow doesn't work once coded" → {{STUDIO_REPO}} (product-designer/ui-designer)
    ds_republish: "Figma DS republished" → design-system-engineer re-syncs tokens + re-checks parity
    build_fail:  build/deploy fails or a {{VERIFY_GATE}} check fails → back to developer, never forward
```

---

## The gates this context adds (beyond the design + usability gates)

1. **Parity gate** (`design-system-engineer`) — the coded component matches its Figma source and is fully
   token-bound (zero drift, zero literals), verified by a Storybook screenshot, not by eye.
2. **{{VERIFY_GATE}} gate** (`developer`) — {{VERIFY_GATE_DESC}}. Proven, not inferred.
3. **Release gate** (`release-engineer`) — verify-by-artifact + honest declarations + the release confirmed
   by re-reading {{RELEASE_CONFIRM}}. A green command is not a shipped release.

These compose with the studio's design-review + usability gates — the same "prove it, don't assume it"
discipline, carried into code and release.

